The N+1 problem, often hidden inside a seemingly innocent loop:

// N+1 PROBLEM - 1 initial query, then N additional queries inside a loop
async function getPostsWithAuthors() {
  const posts = await postsCollection.find().toArray(); // query #1

  for (const post of posts) {
    post.author = await usersCollection.findOne({ _id: post.authorId });
    // ^ THIS runs ONCE PER POST - for 50 posts, that's 50 ADDITIONAL
    // queries, for a total of 51 queries to satisfy ONE conceptual request
  }

  return posts;
}

The fix - batching the author lookup into ONE additional query, directly
extending Module 19's database querying discipline:

async function getPostsWithAuthorsFixed() {
  const posts = await postsCollection.find().toArray(); // query #1

  const authorIds = posts.map((p) => p.authorId);
  const authors = await usersCollection.find({ _id: { $in: authorIds } }).toArray();
  // ^ query #2 - ONE single query fetching ALL needed authors at once,
  // regardless of how many posts there are

  const authorMap = new Map(authors.map((a) => [a._id.toString(), a]));
  posts.forEach((post) => { post.author = authorMap.get(post.authorId.toString()); });

  return posts; // now exactly 2 total queries, regardless of post count
}

