Setting up Real User Monitoring (RUM) using the web-vitals library, sending ACTUAL user data (not just lab measurements) to a monitoring service:

import { onLCP, onINP, onCLS } from 'web-vitals';

function sendToAnalytics(metric: { name: string; value: number }) {
  fetch('/api/metrics', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(metric)
  });
}

onLCP(sendToAnalytics);
onINP(sendToAnalytics);
onCLS(sendToAnalytics);

Setting up an alert that fires automatically when real, aggregated
metrics genuinely degrade beyond an acceptable threshold:

// (Conceptual - actual configuration varies by monitoring provider)
alertRule({
  metric: 'p75_LCP', // the 75th percentile - a genuinely representative
                       // value, more meaningful than a simple average
  threshold: 2500,    // milliseconds, matching lesson 1's "good" threshold
  condition: 'greater_than',
  notify: ['#engineering-alerts']
});

The complete recurring checks from this module:

1. Standardized Measurement (lesson 1) - is performance being measured
   against Core Web Vitals' actual, standardized definitions?
2. Metric-Specific Frontend Fixes (lesson 2) - is each specific
   technique matched to the specific metric it actually improves?
3. Backend Query-Count Awareness (lesson 3) - has the N+1 pattern
   been specifically checked for and ruled out or fixed?
4. Deliberate, Invalidated Caching (lesson 4) - is caching applied
   only where genuinely justified, with correct invalidation?
5. Sequenced Data-Layer Diagnosis (lesson 5) - was the N+1 → index →
   caching sequence followed, with re-measurement at each step?
6. Continuous, Production Monitoring (this lesson) - is real,
   ongoing user data being monitored, with alerts catching genuine
   degradation as it happens, rather than only checking once at launch?

